sec.p:
The governing equations are the three-dimensional compressible Navier–Stokes equations, non-dimensionalised by the free-stream density, free-stream velocity, and chord length of the airfoil

(1)



\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{{\partial \rho }}{{\partial t}} + \frac{{\partial \rho {u_k}}}{{\partial {x_k}}} = 0, 
\end{eqnarray}



(2)



\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{{\partial \rho {u_i}}}{{\partial t}} + \frac{{\partial \left( {\rho {u_i}{u_k} + p{\delta _{ik}}} \right)}}{{\partial {x_k}}} = \frac{1}{\text{Re}}\frac{{\partial {\tau _{ik}}}}{{\partial {x_k}}} + {D_c}{S_i},
\end{eqnarray}



(3)



\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{{\partial e}}{{\partial t}} + \frac{{\partial \left( {\left( {e + p} \right){u_k}} \right)}}{{\partial {x_k}}} = \frac{1}{\text{Re}} \left( \frac{{\partial {u_l}{\tau _{kl}}}}{{\partial {x_k}}} - \frac{\partial q_k}{\partial {x_k}} \right)+ {D_c}{S_k}{u_k},\nonumber\\
\end{eqnarray}



(4)



\begin{eqnarray}
q_k = \frac{\mu }{{\left( {\gamma - 1} \right)\text{Pr}}}\frac{{\partial a^2}}{{ \partial {x_k}}} ,\ {S_i} = {Q_c}{E_i}, 
\end{eqnarray}

where xi, ui, a, ρ, p, e, τij, δij, and t represent the non-dimensional forms of the position vector, velocity vector, speed of sound, density, pressure, energy per unit volume, stress tensor, Kronecker delta, and time, respectively. Re, M∞, and Pr denote the Reynolds, Mach, and Prandtl numbers, respectively. Here, the subscript ∞ denotes the quantity in the free-stream condition. These parameters are defined as follows:

(5)



\begin{eqnarray}
\text{Re} = \frac{{{\rho _\infty }{u_\infty }c}}{{{\mu _\infty }}},\;\text{M}_\infty ^{} = \frac{{{u_\infty }}}{{{a_\infty }}},\; \text{Pr} = \frac{{{\mu _\infty }{c_p}}}{{{\kappa _\infty }}},  
\end{eqnarray}

where μ, c, cp, and κ represent the viscosity, chord length, constant pressure specific heat, and heat conduction coefficient, respectively. The last terms on the right-hand side of Equations (2) and (3) represent the body force effect from the plasma actuator. Detailed descriptions of these terms are presented in the next section.

The magnitude of the body force is represented by multiplying the non-dimensional parameter Dc by Si. Here, Dc is defined as follows:

(6)



\begin{eqnarray}
{D_c} = \frac{{{Q_{c,{\it \rm ref}}}{E_{{\it \rm ref}}}c}}{{{\rho _\infty }u_\infty ^2}},  
\end{eqnarray}

where Qc and E represent the electric charge and electric field, and the subscript ref denotes the reference value. In this equation, $Q_{c,\rm ref}$ and Eref were set as the maximum values of Qc and E in the precomputation of the Suzen model. The effectiveness of the Suzen model has been confirmed by simulating induced flow in a quiescent field and comparing with experimental data for steady (Suzen et al. 2005) and unsteady actuation cases (Aono et al. 2014). Especially, for the unsteady actuation case, the distribution of the induced flow by the Suzen model predicts the experimental results when a strength of excitation (Dc) is set to the appropriate range (Aono et al. 2014).

The induced body force from the plasma actuator changes with time according to the AC frequency. The distribution in the previous section was used as an unsteady body force to simulate the actual effect of the plasma actuator by considering the following formulation:

(7)



\begin{eqnarray}
S\left( {x,y,z,t} \right) = {S_{\rm suzen}}\left( {x,y,z} \right){\sin ^2}\left( {2\pi {F_{\rm base}}t} \right) ,  
\end{eqnarray}

where Ssuzen(x, y, z) is the body force from the Suzen model shown in Figure 2, and Fbase = fbasec/u∞ is the non-dimensional base frequency corresponding to the imposed AC frequency. Here, Ssuzen(x, y, z) is constant for the spanwise direction, thus the body force effect is completely two-dimensional. Since the body force generated by the plasma actuator is assumed to be a push–push type in one cycle, the fluctuation of an unsteady body force is modelled using the square of the sine function (Enloe et al. 2006).

In the burst mode actuation, the burst wave shown in Figure 3 was applied with the formulation of an unsteady body force. The burst ratio (BR) is defined as follows:

(8)



\begin{eqnarray}
{\text{BR}} = \frac{T_{\text{on}}}{T},  
\end{eqnarray}

where T is the period of one burst cycle, and Ton is the actuation duration. Here, BR = 1.0 represents the normal mode actuation. The burst frequency f+ is the frequency of the burst actuation represented as follows:

(9)



\begin{eqnarray}
{f}^{+} = \frac{1}{T}.  
\end{eqnarray}

F+ is the non-dimensional burst frequency defined as follows:

(10)



\begin{eqnarray}
{F}^{+} = \frac{f^{+} c }{u_\infty }.  
\end{eqnarray}


Figure 3.

Diagram of a burst wave.

The computational results are averaged in three ways including station averaging, spanwise averaging, and partial time averaging. The ensemble-averaged value is written as follows:

(11)



\begin{eqnarray}
\langle \phi (j,l,m)\rangle = \frac{1}{N}\sum _{n=N(m-1)+1}^{Nm} \frac{1}{I}\sum _{i=1}^{I} \frac{1}{K}\sum _{k=1}^{K} \phi (i,j,k,l,n),\nonumber\\  
\end{eqnarray}

where φ(i, j, k, l, n) is the instantaneous flow data at the nth time step, i denotes each time station, j, k, l are grid indices, m is the index of the ensemble-averaged value, and I, K, N are the number of samples of each averaging, respectively,

(12)



\begin{eqnarray}
\!\! I=20, \,\,\, K= \left\lfloor \frac{k_{\text{max}}-1}{2} \right \rfloor + 1, \,\,\, N= \left\lfloor \frac{T}{10 \Delta t} \right \rfloor\! ,  
\end{eqnarray}

⌊ ⌋ is the floor function.
